/**
 * @file    pqc_sign_kat_vector.h
 * @brief   Cross-implementation ML-DSA vectors for app_pqc_sign_self_test() (Giai doan 4, see CLAUDE.md).
 *
 * Generated on the PC by the independent Python `dilithium-py` package (FIPS 204 ML-DSA -- an unrelated
 * implementation from unrelated authors to the vendored PQClean C source this firmware runs), the same
 * methodology Giai doan 0 used for pqc_kem_kat_vector.h's ML-KEM-768 vector and for the same reason: no
 * host C compiler was available on the machine that first vendored this code, so NIST's own C KAT harness
 * could not be run locally -- an independent second implementation of the same standard is the next best
 * cross-check, and arguably a MORE meaningful one (it proves interop with someone else's reading of the
 * spec, not just self-consistency).
 *
 * For each level: KAT_<LEVEL>_SEED is a fixed 32-byte seed (no special meaning, just fixed bytes so this
 * vector is reproducible); KAT_<LEVEL>_PK is dilithium-py's ML_DSA_<level>.key_derive(seed)'s public-key
 * output -- app_pqc_sign_self_test() calls pqc_sign_keygen_from_seed() with the same seed and checks the
 * public key it gets back matches bit-for-bit (proves the vendored PQClean C keygen-from-seed path, and
 * this file's pqc_randombytes_pin_next() plumbing, agree with an unrelated Python implementation of FIPS
 * 204's KeyGen). KAT_<LEVEL>_MSG/_SIG are a fixed message and dilithium-py's ML_DSA_<level>.sign(sk, msg,
 * deterministic=True) output (FIPS 204's deterministic signing mode, chosen here ONLY so this vector is
 * reproducible -- firmware itself always signs in the default randomized/hedged mode, see pqc_sign.h) --
 * app_pqc_sign_self_test() calls pqc_sign_verify() on this signature and requires it to accept, proving
 * the vendored PQClean C verify path accepts a signature an unrelated Python implementation produced.
 *
 * Generation script (not checked in -- trivial to reproduce, see this comment):
 *   pip install dilithium-py
 *   from dilithium_py.ml_dsa import ML_DSA_44, ML_DSA_65, ML_DSA_87
 *   seed = bytes([(i*7+3) % 256 for i in range(32)]); pk, sk = ML_DSA_65.key_derive(seed)
 *   sig = ML_DSA_65.sign(sk, msg, deterministic=True)
 */
